<main>
<section class="hero">
  <div>
    <div class="series"><span>01 / 10</span> NBA TIME MACHINE · SERIES PREMIERE</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">ANTICIPATE ORIGINAL RESEARCH / NBA HISTORY</div>
    <h1>We looked for the next <em>John Stockton.</em> The numbers had other ideas.</h1>
    <p class="dek">We searched 279 players for a modern version of one of basketball's greatest passers. Our model picked a winner. That was the easy part.</p>
    <div class="hero-meta"><span>BY <b>DIVINE OSAS-EGBE</b></span><span>STOCKTON: <b>1989–90</b></span><span>MODERN NBA: <b>2025–26</b></span><span><b>7-MIN READ</b></span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="hero-art" role="img" aria-label="Graphic showing Stockton's 57.4 percent assist rate with vintage-inspired basketball court lines">
    <div class="topline"><span>ANTICIPATE / TIME MACHINE</span><span>1989–90 → 2025–26</span></div>
    <div><div class="hero-big">57.4<span style="font-size:.42em;letter-spacing:-2px">%</span></div><div class="hero-caption">OF TEAMMATES' MADE SHOTS ASSISTED*</div></div>
    <div class="bottomline"><span>*WHILE ON COURT · ESTIMATE</span><span>STOCKTON / 01</span></div>
  </div>
</section>
<div class="stat-strip" aria-label="John Stockton 1989–90 season highlights">
  <div><strong>14.5</strong><span>ASSISTS PER GAME<br/>1989–90</span></div>
  <div><strong>1,134</strong><span>TOTAL ASSISTS<br/>IN 78 GAMES</span></div>
  <div><strong>+7.0</strong><span>RELATIVE TRUE SHOOTING<br/>PERCENTAGE POINTS</span></div>
  <div><strong>207</strong><span>STEALS<br/>REGULAR SEASON</span></div>
</div>
<div class="story-layout">
<article class="prose">
<p class="opening">Imagine you're trying to guard a guy who doesn't need to score to make you miserable. Every time you take away one passing lane, he seems to find another. You look up and his teammate has an open layup. Again.</p>
<p>That was the John Stockton problem.</p>
<p>At 6-foot-1 and 170 pounds, he wasn't the biggest player on the floor. He wasn't the flashiest, either. But in 1989–90, Utah's offense kept finding its way through him. Over 78 games he piled up 1,134 assists, averaged 14.5 a night, and still scored an efficient 17.2 points himself.</p>
<p>So here's a fun question: <strong>if Stockton walked into today's NBA, who would he actually resemble?</strong></p>
<p>Not “who's as great as him?” Nobody's asking a 25-year-old to reproduce 19 seasons of work. We wanted to know who looked most like a single, extraordinary Stockton season — in production, size and role.</p>
<p>We gathered every player who logged at least 1,000 minutes in 2025–26. That's 279 players. Then we let the numbers pick their five closest matches.</p>
<div class="highlight">The algorithm gave us an answer. The closer we looked, the less straightforward that answer became.</div>
<h2><span class="section-no">01 / THE SEASON</span>A point guard who barely needed the ball to shoot</h2>
<p>Stockton's 57.4% assist percentage in 1989–90 is almost ridiculous when you stop to think about it. The metric estimates what portion of his teammates' made field goals he assisted while he was on the court. Imagine more than half of those makes having his fingerprints on them.</p>
<p>And here's the part I find even more interesting: his usage rate was just 20.6%. Usage is largely about finishing possessions through shots, free throws or turnovers — it isn't a stopwatch for how long someone holds the ball. Stockton could be at the centre of nearly every action without needing to be the one taking the shot.</p>
<p>His shooting held up, too. He finished 7.0 percentage points above his league's average true shooting. This wasn't a guard racking up assists while giving points back on the other end.</p>
<div class="assist-chart">
  <h3>That assist percentage was a different planet</h3>
  <p>1989–90 Stockton versus selected leaders and matches from our 2025–26 sample. Higher means a larger share of teammates' made baskets assisted while on court.</p>
  {comparisons.map(c=>
    <div class="assist-row">
      <span class="name">{c.name}</span>
      <div class="assist-track" aria-hidden="true"><div class:list={["assist-fill",{"gold":c.type==="legend"}]} style={"width:"+(c.ast/65*100).toFixed(2)+"%"}></div></div>
      <b>{c.ast.toFixed(1)}%</b>
    </div>
  )}
  <p class="chart-foot">Jokić led the 279 qualified modern players in assist percentage (50.3%). Stockton still cleared him by 7.1 percentage points. Assist percentage is a rate estimate, not a literal count of every possession.</p>
</div>
<h2><span class="section-no">02 / THE SEARCH</span>And the closest match is… Kevin Porter Jr.?</h2>
<p>I'll admit it: that's not the first name most people would throw out in a “modern John Stockton” conversation.</p>
<p>But the model doesn't know reputations. It compares seven production measures, adds height and weight, and accounts for broad playing position. It gives 70% weight to statistical output, 20% to physical measurements and 10% to positional group.</p>
<p>Porter finished first, just ahead of Tre Jones and Isaiah Collier. They're close enough that it would be silly to treat that ordering as an ironclad verdict. The fun part is figuring out <em>why</em> each guy ended up there.</p>
</article>
