<div class="story-layout"><article class="prose">
<h2><span class="section-no">04 / THE CONTENDERS</span>One winner, three very different arguments</h2>
<p>Start with <strong>Kevin Porter Jr.</strong> He produced 25.8 points per 100 possessions to Stockton's 23.0. His steal rate was 3.2% against Stockton's 3.5%. That combination gets you into the same statistical neighbourhood.</p>
<p>But Porter is roughly four inches taller and 33 pounds heavier. His 33.8% assist rate is impressive in isolation — until you put it next to Stockton's 57.4%. And Stockton was much more efficient relative to his league (+7.0 rTS to Porter's −1.4).</p>
<p>Now click <strong>Tre Jones</strong> in the graphic. Same listed height as Stockton: 185 cm. Jones used 19.2% of possessions, Stockton 20.6%. Jones' relative true shooting was +5.6, Stockton's +7.0. Suddenly the comparison <em>feels</em> a lot more recognizable. The catch? Jones' assist percentage was 29.2%.</p>
<p>And <strong>Isaiah Collier</strong> makes his own case. He posted a 39.2% assist percentage, the closest of our five model matches. He's not Stockton, but if passing is the first thing you think about, his name deserves a longer look.</p>
<div class="vs-card"><div class="player-side"><span>THE MODEL'S NO. 1</span><strong>Kevin Porter Jr.</strong><em>Closest overall measured profile</em></div><div class="vs">VS</div><div class="player-side"><span>THE BIGGEST WHAT-IF</span><strong>Tre Jones</strong><em>Closer in size, usage and efficiency</em></div></div>
<p>That's what makes this more interesting than a simple “who's the next X?” list. You can get multiple reasonable answers depending on what you think makes Stockton <em>Stockton</em>.</p>
<h2><span class="section-no">05 / THE OUTLIER</span>The one thing no one could copy</h2>
<p>The 2025–26 player who came closest to Stockton's assist percentage wasn't Porter. Or Jones. Or even Collier.</p>
<p>It was <strong>Nikola Jokić</strong>, at 50.3%. Behind him was Cade Cunningham at 45.2%.</p>
<p>There's something wonderfully strange about that. A 6-foot-11 center was the closest modern player to Stockton's share of teammates' assisted baskets, but nobody would look at those two on a court and confuse their styles.</p>
<p>That's the point of including physical profiles in our model. Similar production can come from very different kinds of basketball players. And similar-looking players can do completely different jobs.</p>
<div class="highlight">We found the nearest number. We didn't find the same player.</div>
<h2><span class="section-no">06 / THE VERDICT</span>Maybe there isn't a next Stockton</h2>
<p>The data has an answer: <strong>Kevin Porter Jr.</strong>, with a distance of 1.300 in our first model. Fair enough.</p>
<p>But if you'd rather nominate Tre Jones because of the size and efficiency, I get it. If you'd choose Isaiah Collier for the playmaking, I get that too.</p>
<p>What I'm less willing to do is pretend one season of numbers can capture everything Stockton did. His connection with Karl Malone mattered. The spacing and shot selection of the late '80s mattered. The way Utah built its offense mattered. And assist percentage can't tell you how hard the pass was or how much pressure a defense was under.</p>
<p>When we started this project, I expected the fun part to be seeing a modern name next to a legend. Instead, the interesting part was discovering exactly where the comparison fell apart.</p>
<p>For Stockton, that breaking point was the same thing that made him exceptional in the first place: an almost absurd share of the offense's playmaking, without needing to finish every possession himself.</p>
<p>So yes, the model found a closest match. But the next John Stockton?</p>
<p><strong>I'm not sure we've found him yet.</strong></p>
<div class="end-mark">a.</div>
<section class="method" aria-label="Methodology and sources">
 <h3>THE FINE PRINT / HOW WE DID THIS</h3>
 <p><strong>Sample:</strong> one historical reference season (Stockton, 1989–90) compared with 279 NBA players who played at least 1,000 minutes in 2025–26. Traded players have combined-season rows.</p>
 <p><strong>Model:</strong> mean squared standardized differences in PTS/100, rTS%, AST%, TRB%, USG%, STL% and BLK%, weighted 70%; height and weight differences weighted 20%; broad positional match weighted 10%. Overall distance is the square root of the combined score. Lower is closer. This is similarity, not greatness or player quality.</p>
 <p><strong>Constraints:</strong> single-season comparisons, era differences, official listed weights (not necessarily playing-weight), three-point percentage excluded from scoring, and simplified guard/forward/center roles. Radar charts use fixed plotting ranges, not the exact distance model. The 1989–90 historical inputs should be independently audited before the project is treated as a definitive research result.</p>
 <p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/s/stockjo01.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stockton's season record</a> · <a href="https://www.nba.com/news/history-nba-legend-john-stockton" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NBA history profile</a> · <a href="https://www.basketball-reference.com/leagues/NBA_2026_per_poss.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025–26 per 100 possessions</a> · <a href="https://www.basketball-reference.com/leagues/NBA_2026_advanced.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025–26 advanced stats</a> · <a href="https://github.com/EasySportsApps/nba_api_25_26_data" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NBA Stats-derived bio dataset</a>.</p>
</section>
